    "evidence_text": "Society of the Hoysala Empire\n\n It was built in Halebidu (in modern Karnataka state, India) during the rule of King Vishnuvardhana of the Hoysala Empire in the 12th century.\n\n During the early 14th century, Halebidu was sacked and looted by Muslim invaders from northern India and the temple fell into a state of ruin and neglect.\n\n Previously known as Dorasamudra or Dwarasamudra, Halebidu is 16\u00a0km from Belur, 31\u00a0km from Hassan and 149\u00a0km from Mysore, in the state of Karnataka, India.\n\n Halebidu is historically important as the erstwhile capital of the Hoysala empire.\n\nHalebidu\n\n Halebidu (which used to be called Dorasamudra or Dwarasamudra) was the regal capital of the Hoysala Empire in the 12th century.\n\n The city got the name \"Halebidu\" because it was damaged and deserted into \"old capital\" after being ransacked and looted twice by north Indian Muslim armies of the Delhi Sultanate in the 14th-century.\n\nHoysala architecture",
